<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Allegory of Good and Bad Government</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1338–<a href="/years/1339">1339</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Allegory of Good and Bad Government is a cycle of frescoes painted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrogio-lorenzetti">Ambrogio Lorenzetti</a> in 1338–1339 in the Palazzo Pubblico of Siena, in the room where the city's governing council of the Nine met. It contrasts the virtues of just republican rule, shown in a prosperous city and countryside, with the ruin brought by tyranny. It is one of the most important works of secular political art of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/allegory-of-good-and-bad-government.jpg" alt="The Allegory of Good and Bad Government" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ambrogio Lorenzetti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ambrogio_Lorenzetti_-_Allegory_of_the_Good_Government_(detail)_-_WGA13487.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The series was commissioned by the Council of Nine, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrogio-lorenzetti">Ambrogio Lorenzetti</a> worked on it between February 1338 and May 1339. Its subject is civic rather than religious, unusual for the art of the period, and it was painted at a turbulent time for Italian cities, marked by violent party struggles and the overthrow of governments. The three fresco panels cover three of the four walls of the Sala dei Nove: the south wall holds the room's only window, the north wall faces it with the virtues of good government, and the effects of good and bad government face each other on the east and west walls.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>In the Allegory of Good Government a crowned figure dressed in the black and white of the Balzana, Siena's coat of arms, and marked with the letters C S C V, represents the Commune. Faith, Hope and Charity hover above him, and around him sit Peace, Fortitude, Prudence, Magnanimity, Temperance and Justice. Below, citizens holding two woven cords that gather beneath the scales of Justice stand for the city's officers. Two children at the ruler's feet may be Ascius and Senius, the sons of Remus who according to Roman legend founded Siena.</p><p>The Effects of Good Government gives a panorama of a thriving city, with shops, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> members at work, a wedding procession and dancers, opening through a gate onto the Tuscan countryside, where the winged figure of Security holds a scroll promising safe travel. Medallions of the planets and seasons run along the borders, and the labours of the months appear in the scenes. Opposite, a horned Tyranny sits enthroned with his feet on a goat, Justice lies bound below him, and the city falls into ruin while two armies advance across the countryside.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Scholars disagree whether the city is Siena itself, pointing to its bell tower, domed cathedral and a small port labelled Telamon, or an ideal city that departs from the real one. The skewed perspective has been explained as radiating from a point in front of Tyranny or, more often, as following the gaze of Justice; photographs taken along her line of sight show the buildings in correct perspective. The ten dancers wear <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> although Sienese statutes of 1338 banned public dancing and restricted silk. The Bad Government fresco has been studied less, partly because moisture has badly damaged its former exterior wall.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Allegory of Good and Bad Government” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From 1287 to 1355 the merchant government of the Nine built the Palazzo Pubblico, where Ambrogio Lorenzetti painted his <b>Allegory of Good and Bad Government</b> in 1338–1339.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-siena">Republic of Siena</a></li></ul></section>
